1
00:00:02,230 --> 00:00:06,690
Hello English learners, welcome back to another great podcast here at EnglishPod.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:07,470
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:08,390
And I'm Erica.

4
00:00:08,930 --> 00:00:12,450
And today we're going to be talking about one of our favorite topics as always.

5
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:13,960
Food.

6
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:14,960
Food.

7
00:00:14,960 --> 00:00:15,960
We're going to be at a restaurant.

8
00:00:15,960 --> 00:00:25,110
And, you know, one of the most difficult or embarrassing things to deal with at a restaurant is what to say when you don't like the food or when it's bad quality food.

9
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:25,670
Right.

10
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:29,710
So maybe we have to complain about the food or maybe even the service, right?

11
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:30,250
That's right.

12
00:00:30,470 --> 00:00:39,730
And in this lesson, we're going to be learning some really wonderful words and phrases that you can use when you need to complain about the food or the service in a restaurant.

13
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:40,650
Okay.

14
00:00:40,710 --> 00:00:48,090
So before we listen to what happens in this restaurant with this food, let's take a look very quickly at vocabulary preview.

15
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:50,880
Vocabulary preview.

16
00:00:51,210 --> 00:00:51,690
Okay.

17
00:00:51,690 --> 00:00:53,290
We're only going to do one word today.

18
00:00:53,630 --> 00:00:55,630
And this you'll hear in the dialogue.

19
00:00:56,310 --> 00:00:57,390
An establishment.

20
00:00:57,640 --> 00:00:58,640
Establishment.

21
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:00,790
This kind of establishment.

22
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:01,770
Okay.

23
00:01:01,930 --> 00:01:04,430
So when I say an establishment, that's a noun.

24
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,080
What is an establishment?

25
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:10,930
It's basically a fancy word for a business or a restaurant in this case.

26
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:12,190
In this case, it's a restaurant.

27
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:13,360
Yeah.

28
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:14,190
But in general, you can say it's a business.

29
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:17,360
So you have a very fine establishment.

30
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:18,360
That's right.

31
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:22,320
So an establishment, a type of business, a place of business, really.

32
00:01:23,040 --> 00:01:23,200
Very good.

33
00:01:24,040 --> 00:01:25,040
All right.

34
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:26,840
So now it's time for us to listen to our dialogue.

35
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:28,720
We're going to find out what's going on here.

36
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:33,120
Apparently, there is a very angry customer and we're going to find out why.

37
00:01:40,010 --> 00:01:40,670
Excuse me, waiter.

38
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:42,140
Yes, sir.

39
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:43,140
What can I do for you?

40
00:01:43,380 --> 00:01:49,980
I've been sitting here for the past 20 minutes and no one has offered me a glass of water, brought any bread to the table, and our appetizers haven't been served yet.

41
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:53,240
You know, in this kind of establishment, I'd expect much better service.

42
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:54,320
I'm sorry, sir.

43
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:55,800
I'll check on your order right away.

44
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:57,180
Relax, honey.

45
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:00,620
The place is busy tonight and I've heard the food is amazing.

46
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:01,600
Anyway.

47
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:02,540
Here you are, sir.

48
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:06,240
The foie gras for the lady and a mushroom soup for you.

49
00:02:08,150 --> 00:02:08,550
Waiter!

50
00:02:09,740 --> 00:02:11,900
I ordered a cream of mushroom soup with asparagus.

51
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:15,000
The soup is obviously too runny and it's over seasoned.

52
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,000
It's completely inedible.

53
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:16,680
Okay.

54
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:18,060
I do apologize for that.

55
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:20,460
Can I bring you another soup or would you like to order something else?

56
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:22,060
Take this foie gras back as well.

57
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:23,880
It's rubbery and completely overcooked.

58
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:25,300
And look at the portion size.

59
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:28,200
How can you charge $25 for a sliver of duck liver?

60
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:29,780
Right away, sir.

61
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:30,960
Honey, come on.

62
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:31,960
The foie gras was fine.

63
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:34,800
Why are you making such a big deal?

64
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:37,620
Are you trying to get our meal comped again?

65
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:38,900
What do you mean?

66
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:41,720
We're paying for this and I'm shelling out my hard earned bucks.

67
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:43,700
I expect value for money.

68
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:45,160
Here you are, sir.

69
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:46,900
I hope it is all right now.

70
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:48,780
The chef has prepared it especially for you.

71
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:49,420
Yes, fine.

72
00:02:50,120 --> 00:03:16,280
Honey, are you all right?

73
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:17,820
Well I guess that's one lesson.

74
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:20,960
Hey, if you're in a restaurant, don't be too rude to the waiter.

75
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:22,240
You never know what's going to happen.

76
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:24,440
The waiter and the cook, right?

77
00:03:24,740 --> 00:03:27,100
Yeah, you don't want to offend that kitchen staff.

78
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:28,560
All right.

79
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:42,760
So we'll talk about that a little bit later on but now I think we had some really interesting vocabulary of how to describe food that is not well prepared and we can take a look at that now in Language Takeaway.

80
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:45,820
Language Takeaway.

81
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:54,240
All right, so the guy in this dialogue, he ordered a mushroom soup and he complained that the mushroom soup was too runny.

82
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:56,300
Okay, the soup is too runny.

83
00:03:56,420 --> 00:03:56,800
Runny.

84
00:03:57,260 --> 00:04:01,020
So he wanted a cream of mushroom so it should be a little bit thicker.

85
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:01,560
Yep.

86
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:03,710
But in this case, it was runny.

87
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:05,870
So when something is runny, it's too liquid.

88
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:06,850
Too liquid.

89
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:10,010
And I think we can relate it to a runny nose, right?

90
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:11,480
Exactly.

91
00:04:11,480 --> 00:04:16,360
Okay, so when liquid is coming out of your nose, a runny nose, in this case, the soup is runny.

92
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:17,360
Right.

93
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:21,090
And another thing that he was complaining about, the food was over seasoned.

94
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:22,760
Over seasoned.

95
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:24,320
The food is over seasoned.

96
00:04:24,410 --> 00:04:27,470
Okay, so if the food is over seasoned.

97
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:28,240
It's too salty.

98
00:04:28,700 --> 00:04:30,640
So it's another way of saying it's too salty.

99
00:04:30,820 --> 00:04:33,480
Yeah, it's kind of a fancy cooking term.

100
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,660
Now can I say if there's too much pepper in the food, can I say it's over seasoned?

101
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:40,200
Well, technically no.

102
00:04:40,560 --> 00:04:44,200
When a cook says something is over seasoned, he's talking salt only.

103
00:04:44,460 --> 00:04:45,040
Only salt.

104
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:52,220
So the cream of mushroom is very runny, it's over seasoned, and he said it's completely inedible.

105
00:04:52,600 --> 00:04:53,120
Inedible.

106
00:04:53,820 --> 00:04:54,160
It's inedible.

107
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:55,100
Inedible.

108
00:04:56,350 --> 00:04:57,510
Now, this word edible.

109
00:04:58,330 --> 00:04:59,050
You can eat it.

110
00:04:59,370 --> 00:04:59,930
You can eat it.

111
00:05:00,050 --> 00:05:02,410
So we have this prefix inedible.

112
00:05:02,810 --> 00:05:03,930
You cannot eat it.

113
00:05:03,990 --> 00:05:04,950
This is not possible.

114
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:06,080
Yep.

115
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:07,970
And it doesn't mean that physically you can't eat it.

116
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:09,330
It just means it's pretty disgusting.

117
00:05:09,710 --> 00:05:10,010
Okay.

118
00:05:10,510 --> 00:05:11,910
This food is inedible.

119
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:17,710
And then he started to complain about the other dish and he said that it's rubbery.

120
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:18,650
Rubbery.

121
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:21,680
The foie gras was rubbery.

122
00:05:22,090 --> 00:05:22,310
All right.

123
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:24,810
So I think we noticed the word rubber.

124
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:31,210
So obviously if a food is rubbery, it's too chewy.

125
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:33,990
It's like chewing on the bottom of your shoe, right?

126
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:37,190
Okay, so it's too hard to chew or to swallow.

127
00:05:37,590 --> 00:05:37,850
Exactly.

128
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:39,680
All right.

129
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:43,320
And apparently the food was rubbery because it's completely overcooked.

130
00:05:43,870 --> 00:05:44,320
Overcooked.

131
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:45,720
All right.

132
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:46,890
We have overcooked.

133
00:05:47,430 --> 00:05:51,770
So when something is overcooked, I mean it's easy to understand that it's cooked too much.

134
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:54,190
But it doesn't mean that it's burnt, right?

135
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:54,890
No.

136
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:59,470
So maybe you've ordered your steak to be rare, but it comes to you well done.

137
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:02,250
So it's not burnt, but it's cooked too much.

138
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:04,550
So also I guess we can say like chicken.

139
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:07,610
Sometimes if you overcook chicken, it comes out too dry.

140
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:08,880
Yep.

141
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:09,880
Right.

142
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:10,410
So to overcook something.

143
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:20,110
And finally he was complaining about the size of the dish and he said, this is a sliver of duck liver.

144
00:06:20,560 --> 00:06:20,870
Okay.

145
00:06:21,930 --> 00:06:22,450
A sliver.

146
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:23,560
A sliver.

147
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:23,810
All right.

148
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:26,670
Now this word sliver is like a slice, right?

149
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:29,710
A very small, tiny little slice.

150
00:06:30,150 --> 00:06:30,470
Okay.

151
00:06:30,570 --> 00:06:34,410
So we can say, okay, I just want a sliver of bacon for breakfast.

152
00:06:34,770 --> 00:06:34,990
All right.

153
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:39,440
Let's look at a few more examples of how we can use sliver.

154
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:39,730
Example one.

155
00:06:41,930 --> 00:06:43,610
No, John's on a diet.

156
00:06:44,170 --> 00:06:46,510
He'll just have a sliver of cake and no ice cream.

157
00:06:48,590 --> 00:06:49,510
Example two.

158
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:53,460
I'm really full, but it looks so delicious.

159
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:55,460
Can you just cut me a sliver?

160
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:00,140
Example three.

161
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:02,460
You call this sliver of beef on my plate a meal?

162
00:07:03,240 --> 00:07:04,260
There's almost nothing here.

163
00:07:06,050 --> 00:07:08,410
And those are all the words we had for language takeaway.

164
00:07:08,890 --> 00:07:14,050
Now you have, so we've given you different adjectives of describing food that is not well prepared.

165
00:07:14,630 --> 00:07:19,910
So now let's move on to some other phrases that the customer used to complain in Fluency Builder.

166
00:07:21,450 --> 00:07:22,330
Fluency Builder.

167
00:07:23,060 --> 00:07:23,540
Okay.

168
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:30,520
So before we get to the customer complaining language, I want to look at a phrase that the waiter used to apologize.

169
00:07:31,020 --> 00:07:32,900
He said, I do apologize.

170
00:07:33,700 --> 00:07:34,840
I do apologize.

171
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:35,700
I do apologize.

172
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:38,240
Now, why is this phrase so interesting?

173
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:44,480
Well, I mean, it's pretty easy to understand the meaning, but I want to look at why he added this verb do.

174
00:07:45,570 --> 00:07:54,250
Well, he added the verb do to this phrase to make it seem a little bit stronger, to give a little more power to his apology.

175
00:07:54,870 --> 00:07:55,150
Right.

176
00:07:55,760 --> 00:08:00,800
So if you have an affirmative phrase, like I apologize, it's good.

177
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:04,830
If you want to give it a little bit more emphasis, you can say, I do apologize.

178
00:08:05,450 --> 00:08:06,330
Why don't we give an example?

179
00:08:06,810 --> 00:08:10,910
So I might say this, Marco, I didn't know we had a party tonight.

180
00:08:11,590 --> 00:08:13,200
And I might say, what?

181
00:08:13,540 --> 00:08:15,260
I did tell you about it last week.

182
00:08:15,740 --> 00:08:15,900
Right.

183
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:20,220
So you're just giving a little bit more power to the statement that you told me.

184
00:08:21,080 --> 00:08:28,280
So this do, did, does, becomes an auxiliary to make it more powerful, to give it more emphasis.

185
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:29,280
Yeah, exactly.

186
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:35,560
And a neat little phrase, something you can use if you want to make your apologies sound maybe a little bit stronger.

187
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:36,920
All right.

188
00:08:36,920 --> 00:08:41,880
Now moving on to the next phrase, he was complaining about the food and it was terrible.

189
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:45,640
And he said, I'm shelling out my hard earned bucks.

190
00:08:45,640 --> 00:08:48,760
Okay, let's break this one down into two sections.

191
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:50,680
We'll start with shelling out.

192
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:51,800
All right.

193
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:53,060
So to shell out.

194
00:08:53,060 --> 00:08:55,440
So when you shell out, you pay for something.

195
00:08:55,440 --> 00:08:56,440
All right.

196
00:08:56,440 --> 00:09:00,960
So this gives you the sensation that you are, that you don't really want to pay.

197
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:02,100
You're a bit unhappy about it.

198
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:02,760
Right.

199
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:04,760
So you want to shell out your money.

200
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:10,380
Why don't we listen to some examples to show us how we can use this phrase to shell out.

201
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:13,200
Example one.

202
00:09:14,330 --> 00:09:14,770
Great.

203
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:16,770
It's my girlfriend's birthday this month.

204
00:09:17,110 --> 00:09:18,750
So I'm going to have to shell out for a present.

205
00:09:20,960 --> 00:09:21,190
Example two.

206
00:09:23,130 --> 00:09:26,650
I really want those shoes, but I can't really afford to shell out $400.

207
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:30,540
Example three.

208
00:09:32,410 --> 00:09:33,490
I can't believe it.

209
00:09:34,210 --> 00:09:36,130
Ella shelled out the 800 bucks for the tickets.

210
00:09:36,710 --> 00:09:37,610
I thought I would have to pay.

211
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:39,720
All right.

212
00:09:40,560 --> 00:09:43,780
So now that we understand shell out, let's move on to the second part of that sentence.

213
00:09:44,360 --> 00:09:45,700
My hard earned bucks.

214
00:09:46,680 --> 00:09:47,440
Hard earned bucks.

215
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:49,280
My hard earned bucks.

216
00:09:49,620 --> 00:09:49,840
Okay.

217
00:09:49,900 --> 00:09:51,580
So bucks are dollars, right?

218
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:53,080
Dollars, only dollars, right?

219
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:54,360
Yeah.

220
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:54,940
Not pounds, not euros.

221
00:09:56,320 --> 00:10:00,740
But his hard earned bucks is money he had to work really hard for.

222
00:10:01,680 --> 00:10:04,560
So I can also say my hard earned money, right?

223
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:06,780
Or my hard earned cash.

224
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:08,320
Exactly.

225
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:08,710
Very good.

226
00:10:09,320 --> 00:10:10,550
So you worked hard to get it.

227
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:11,880
All right.

228
00:10:11,880 --> 00:10:15,370
So three great phrases that can be used when you're complaining in a restaurant.

229
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:19,750
Why don't we hear them one last time in context by listening to the dialogue.

230
00:10:26,050 --> 00:10:26,850
Excuse me, waiter.

231
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:28,300
Yes, sir.

232
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:29,300
What can I do for you?

233
00:10:29,620 --> 00:10:36,160
I've been sitting here for the past 20 minutes and no one has offered me a glass of water, brought any bread to the table, and our appetizers haven't been served yet.

234
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:39,420
You know, in this kind of establishment, I'd expect much better service.

235
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:40,500
I'm sorry, sir.

236
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:41,980
I'll check on your order right away.

237
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:43,320
Relax, honey.

238
00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:46,820
The place is busy tonight and I've heard the food is amazing.

239
00:10:47,080 --> 00:10:48,080
Anyway.

240
00:10:48,100 --> 00:10:48,720
Here you are, sir.

241
00:10:49,080 --> 00:10:52,400
The foie gras for the lady and a mushroom soup for you.

242
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:53,400
Waiter!

243
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:58,280
I ordered a cream of mushroom soup with asparagus.

244
00:10:58,280 --> 00:11:00,420
The soup is obviously too runny and it's over seasoned.

245
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:02,240
It's completely inedible.

246
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:04,220
Okay, I do apologize for that.

247
00:11:04,380 --> 00:11:06,620
Can I bring you another soup or would you like to order something else?

248
00:11:06,860 --> 00:11:08,240
Take this foie gras back as well.

249
00:11:08,440 --> 00:11:10,040
It's rubbery and completely overcooked.

250
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:11,440
And look at the portion size.

251
00:11:11,900 --> 00:11:14,380
How can you charge $25 for a sliver of duck liver?

252
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:15,940
Right away, sir.

253
00:11:16,680 --> 00:11:17,600
Honey, come on.

254
00:11:17,980 --> 00:11:19,260
The foie gras was fine.

255
00:11:19,540 --> 00:11:20,960
Why are you making such a big deal?

256
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:23,800
Are you trying to get our meal comped again?

257
00:11:24,380 --> 00:11:25,060
What do you mean?

258
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:28,440
We're paying for this and I'm shelling out my hard earned bucks.

259
00:11:28,520 --> 00:11:29,880
I expect value for money.

260
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:31,340
Here you are, sir.

261
00:11:31,940 --> 00:11:33,080
I hope it is all right now.

262
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,960
The chef has prepared it especially for you.

263
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:35,580
Yes, fine.

264
00:11:41,640 --> 00:11:43,420
Honey, are you all right?

265
00:12:02,630 --> 00:12:08,650
So complaining at a restaurant and actually sending food back is not uncommon in the US.

266
00:12:08,990 --> 00:12:09,510
Yeah, of course.

267
00:12:09,810 --> 00:12:11,690
If the food's bad, of course you're going to send it back.

268
00:12:12,090 --> 00:12:17,750
But maybe in other cultures, I think people would be a little bit more reluctant to do something like this.

269
00:12:17,950 --> 00:12:18,090
Yeah.

270
00:12:18,650 --> 00:12:27,570
But hey, you know what's an interesting thing about the way Americans eat in restaurants is sometimes at the end of the meal, if they haven't finished everything, they might ask for a doggy bag.

271
00:12:27,930 --> 00:12:28,790
A doggy bag, yeah.

272
00:12:29,290 --> 00:12:30,690
So what is that, a doggy bag?

273
00:12:30,990 --> 00:12:43,070
Well, usually if you order a big meal or half your steak is left over on your plate, you would ask for a doggy bag and they would bring you a little box or a bag and you would take it to supposedly give it to your dog.

274
00:12:43,450 --> 00:12:43,570
Right.

275
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:50,610
So it's sort of like you tell the waiter, oh, the steak was so good, I couldn't finish it, can I take some home to my dog?

276
00:12:50,850 --> 00:12:50,970
Right.

277
00:12:51,230 --> 00:12:53,210
So you would just ask, waiter, can I have a doggy bag?

278
00:12:53,430 --> 00:12:53,550
Right.

279
00:12:54,070 --> 00:12:58,210
But it's sort of like code for I'm going to eat this tomorrow for lunch.

280
00:12:58,450 --> 00:12:58,730
Exactly.

281
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:02,120
So people wouldn't really give it to their dog.

282
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:08,560
You know, that steak wrapped up in a fancy sort of foil salon is going to be a pretty delicious the next day.

283
00:13:08,560 --> 00:13:09,560
Right.

284
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:10,560
Yeah.

285
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:15,560
It's actually very common in the US, but in other countries, people will leave entire plates of food on the table.

286
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:19,280
It would be totally impolite to ask the waiter to pack up the food for you.

287
00:13:19,280 --> 00:13:20,280
Exactly.

288
00:13:20,280 --> 00:13:30,420
So a little bit of cultural differences, but we want to know what you think and how things are done maybe in your country with complaining or sending food back or even this whole doggy bag situation.

289
00:13:31,140 --> 00:13:31,440
That's right.

290
